{"id": "EUVD-2026-82004", "enisaUuid": "b6c18564-70b7-3bd3-8d2c-203d74f71ab9", "description": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nwifi: cfg80211: stop PMSR before P2P and NAN teardown\n\nPMSR request teardown must abort active measurements while the\nwireless_dev is still present in the driver. cfg80211_leave_locked() and\ncfg80211_stop_pd() already do this before invoking the driver's stop\ncallback, but cfg80211_stop_p2p_device() and cfg80211_stop_nan() do not.\n\nThose helpers are also called directly by nl80211, rfkill shutdown, and\nwireless_dev unregister paths. If one of these paths stops a P2P device\nor NAN interface with a pending request, it removes the mac80211\nsubinterface from the driver first. Subsequent request cleanup cannot\nreach the lower driver's abort callback, but cfg80211 frees the request\nregardless. Driver state can then retain a stale request and use it when\nit later reports a result.\n\nCall cfg80211_pmsr_wdev_down() before stopping the P2P device or NAN\ninterface. This keeps lower-driver request state and cfg80211 request\nownership in sync for all of the helpers' callers.", "datePublished": "2026-09-17T16:08:53", "dateUpdated": "2026-10-21T12:51:08", "baseScore": 7.8, "baseScoreVersion": "3.1", "baseScoreVector": "CVSS:3.1/AV:L/AC:L/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:H", "references": ["https://git.kernel.org/stable/c/9374943fc5a45915e4ea8b597b6a17d3b571bcbf", "https://git.kernel.org/stable/c/dc1e41df6e683ff9522bf40dd3125ed7ccd8a4e4", "https://git.kernel.org/stable/c/7a22cbc6c6bdd3c3811b4ce13875685c520f94de", "https://git.kernel.org/stable/c/db3439ad11ac5e52decdefe784c98b21c3757848", "https://git.kernel.org/stable/c/6c5fc504d0d6934132637aa3db4b9b58148eaa78"], "aliases": ["GHSA-vfv4-5w5q-jr9p", "CVE-2026-90343"], "assigner": "Linux", "epss": 0.18, "enisaIdProduct": [{"id": "08cf6185-98c4-3441-90e5-dff2d1c5e22d", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "9bb7e0f24e7e7d00daa1219b14539e2e602649b2 <6c5fc504d0d6934132637aa3db4b9b58148eaa78"}, {"id": "107fc981-b47b-33c1-bc51-7a79ff44f8eb", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "9bb7e0f24e7e7d00daa1219b14539e2e602649b2 <db3439ad11ac5e52decdefe784c98b21c3757848"}, {"id": "11916e2a-28b2-3f3f-a8a6-589a1de6d662", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "patch: 7.2.6"}, {"id": "15a7f07e-db37-37e3-9a9e-bca79e87cda5", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "patch: 6.18.52"}, {"id": "2a76e95e-9f0b-350f-ab84-eca60fe80d5d", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "9bb7e0f24e7e7d00daa1219b14539e2e602649b2 <9374943fc5a45915e4ea8b597b6a17d3b571bcbf"}, {"id": "49ba99b8-1d06-3ff8-a453-a5093f16a888", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "patch: 6.6.160"}, {"id": "757a7f82-e7dd-38ed-b719-413ea2f22f27", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "9bb7e0f24e7e7d00daa1219b14539e2e602649b2 <7a22cbc6c6bdd3c3811b4ce13875685c520f94de"}, {"id": "91d09c3d-c7dc-3883-b578-574ddfc9f188", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "patch: 7.3"}, {"id": "ccc27499-34a3-38db-9c05-5928b35ae2d2", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "5.0"}, {"id": "d2dbeeee-32c5-3a4d-9a40-32feb7c5ab15", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "patch: 6.12.114"}, {"id": "e5db6cc9-db39-3cb8-a6a7-e7724a80c686", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "9bb7e0f24e7e7d00daa1219b14539e2e602649b2 <dc1e41df6e683ff9522bf40dd3125ed7ccd8a4e4"}, {"id": "f5769778-9a8f-34dd-bba1-e4b1d20aa270", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "patch: 0"}], "enisaIdVendor": [{"id": "91b703c8-57c1-37e0-8497-6ae1e5c27d8b", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}], "i